         horizon. Small — six pixels a step — but it stops the row reading as
         one flat baseline. */
      bottom: var(--lift, 0);
      width: var(--w, 0);
      height: var(--h, 0);
      /* Opaque, because the occlusion is real: the card in front physically
         covers the back view of the card behind it. A transparent card would
         let both views show through each other. */
      background: var(--pf-stage);
      /* The depth cue. Now that every studio ground resolves to the same white,
         the overlap has no edge of its own and five figures on one white field
         read as five figures, not as a deck. This is the shadow the card in
         front casts onto the card behind — cast leftward, because that is the
         side it covers. It is the only thing drawn at the seam: no rule, no
         border, no separator. */
      box-shadow: -18px 0 26px -16px rgba(17, 17, 15, .22);
      transition: left .58s var(--ease), width .58s var(--ease), height .58s var(--ease),
